WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.340
Good morning, John.
It’s time for another Bizarre Beasts!

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.150
This is a project I’m doing over the next twelve months
where every month I talk about

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.860
a weird animal in the world,

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.520
and this time I get to talk about a weird
animal who I know personally!

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:17.020
In a way, this is the animal who inspired this idea,
because back many years ago on SciShow,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.520
he came to visit me, and I -
one, I looked very - I looked younger.

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:29.080
And two, I freaked out because, like, if a spaceship
in my backyard, and this thing came out,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.920
that would seem perfectly normal, aside from
there being a spaceship in my backyard.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.120
He is a prehensile-tailed porcupine,
of which there are many species.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.500
This is a Brazilian porcupine in particular.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.240
“I… have not ever seen anything... like that.”

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.760
And even more specifically, this individual is Kemosabe,
who lives with my friends Jessi and Augusto

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.820
here in Montana at Animal Wonders,
which is a permanent home and safe haven

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.930
for exotic animals and native animals
that can’t return to the wild.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.340
Now, Kemo is a bit of an internet celebrity,
partially because of how he looks.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:06.220
There’s just that giant tail and there’s all the spines
and that very good, weird marshmallow nose.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.820
But also because of how he sounds.
Now, there are cuter animals in the world,

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.520
but I don’t know if there are cuter-sounding animals.
Let’s take a minute, just give it a listen.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.420
[COOING SOUNDS FROM KEMOSABE]

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.340
[JESSI:] Why don’t you just listen to him?

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.060
It’s a little bit reminiscent of like
a baby cooing.

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:27.590
Also, of…

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.800
[YOSHI NOISES]

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.600
Right?

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.360
But they also just like sound kind of human, like this
is a noise that would be easy for me to make.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.580
[IMITATES KEMOSABE COOING SOUNDS]

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:45.219
And hearing like this human sound come out of this
very not human-looking organism is, you know,

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:48.149
a little bit exhilarating, it’s very cute.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:50.489
It’s also a little unsettling.

00:01:50.489 --> 00:01:54.280
And those emotions don’t often
come together at the same time.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.200
Animal Wonders has a YouTube channel
at Animal Wonders Montana

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.660
that we actually help produce at Complexly.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.700
It’s wonderful and so adorable, and you
can learn so much! Go subscribe to it.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.140
There’s links all over the place,
wherever you might think you’d find one.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.700
Now for a weird thing about “porcupines.”

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.820
That word includes a number of different species.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.200
They include the ones that we have here in the U.S.
and also down in Central and South America,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.240
like prehensile-tailed porcupines.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.840
And then there are the European
and African and Asian porcupines,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.500
the Old World porcupines, and they are
TOTALLY NOT RELATED!

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.520
I mean they are, they’re both rodents,
but their quills evolved separately.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.260
They evolved from a common ancestor
that didn’t have quills.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.879
They’re only both called “porcupines”
because they’re both… spine pigs.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:46.060
Like pig… pork. Spine… pine.
Porkpine…

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.660
That’s really where their name comes from.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.240
They’re thorn pigs!
[SQUEES]

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.500
But because their quills evolved separately,
they actually function completely differently!

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.409
And it isn’t the only time this has happened.
Hedgehogs and echidna also have spines

00:02:59.409 --> 00:03:04.340
that evolved completely separately because, like, it’s
just not a terrible idea to be really unpleasant to bite.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.660
Prehensile-tailed porcupines live in trees.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.820
That’s why they have their prehensile tails,
and they are very good climbers.

00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:16.410
Indeed, when a porcupette is born (and yes,
that is the proper word for a baby porcupine)

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:22.230
they are soft and fuzzy and can’t very well
be carried around by their big, thorny mamas,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:29.329
so they have the same muscular tails and functioning
claws as their mamas, and they can climb from day one!

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:34.200
I’m very happy to have been able to feature one of
my good friends on - in the Bizarre Beasts segment.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.980
Everybody go subscribe to Animal Wonders.
The work they do is amazing!

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.990
They’re such wonderful people,
and their videos are very good.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.660
And yes, I know my company does produce them.
I’m biased! Who cares!

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.420
Thanks everybody for a wonderful
Project for Awesome.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.719
We had over a thousand more donors
than we had in previous years.

00:03:48.719 --> 00:03:52.980
We beat all of our previous Project for Awesomes.
It was such an amazing time!

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.040
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

